## Context (discovered during 0501 manuscript→177 reconciliation)

`main.md` §4 line 90 states the matched-row attribute accuracies as:
"mean fuel accuracy 0.53, mean status accuracy 0.53, mean province accuracy 0.61".
The abstract frames this as "attribute classification ... is mediocre even for
correctly identified plants."

The current committed artifact `experiments/derived/exp1_cross_eval.csv` (70 runs)
re-derives:

| attribute | prose | artifact (HEAD, 177) | artifact (pre-#906, 180) |
|---|---|---|---|
| fuel     | 0.53 | 0.540 | 0.535 |
| status   | 0.53 | 0.574 | 0.569 |
| province | 0.61 | **0.889** | **0.892** |

## Investigation — root cause is prose staleness, NOT a scoring change (settled)

Initial hypothesis was an upstream province-scoring change. **Disproven.**

- Province was already **0.892 at the 180-plant reference** (commit `ce4fa14~1`,
  before #906) and 0.889 after the 177 re-score. So the 177 re-score moved it
  by −0.003, not +0.28. #906 is not the cause.
- The province-cleaning path (`reconcile.py` `_lookup_attrs` →
  `province_clean` via `PowerPlantDataframeCleaner`) has been **stable since
  #840** (`763b1c8`, ticket 0395). No scoring bug.
- `province_match` is only set when BOTH ref and sys carry a province
  (`reconcile.py:214-215`); empty/None pairs are excluded, so 0.89 is a sound
  metric over matched-and-attributed rows.
- The prose "0.61 / 0.53 / 0.53" was hand-typed at the **16-model / 80-run**
  vintage (`763b1c8`, Jun 8). On the migration to **14 models / 70 runs**
  (`9bc7e91`, which dropped two unreliable models), the author refreshed F1,
  the within-model range, and per-model means — but **froze the three
  attribute accuracies**. Dropping the two worst models lifted province
  recovery sharply (fuel/status barely moved; province moved most), and the
  prose never caught up. `git log -G "province accuracy 0\."` confirms the
  "0.61" string is byte-identical across every commit from `763b1c8` to #906.

## The actual finding (and why it needs the author)

At 0.89, **province is well-recovered, not mediocre.** The blanket claim
"attribute classification (fuel, status, province) is mediocre even for
correctly identified plants" (abstract + §4 line 90) is **false for province**.
The honest read: *fuel (0.54) and status (0.57) classification stay mediocre;
province (0.89) is recovered well* — plausibly because Vietnamese provinces are
a small, famous controlled vocabulary a model that recalls a plant usually
places correctly, whereas fuel and status carry finer distinctions models
conflate. This is a genuine sub-finding, not a disappointment — so the author
rewrites the claim rather than a script re-typing a number.

## Definition of done

1. Author ratifies the corrected line-90 numbers (fuel 0.54 / status 0.57 /
   province 0.89) and the reworded §4 + abstract framing that carves province
   out of the "mediocre" claim.
2. Update `main.md` line 90 + abstract accordingly.
3. Add an adherence guard (like 0501's `test_manuscript_derived_numbers.py`)
   re-deriving the three attribute means from `exp1_cross_eval.csv` so they
   cannot drift unguarded again — this is the gap that froze 0.61 for a month.
4. `make check` green.

## Out of scope

- 0501 (count/fuel/difficulty/§6 reconciliation — merged separately).

